// The Appeal, ch4: Weight. Saturday, January 16 to Thursday, January 28, 2027.
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR07x4 LR08x4 RC13x2 RC04x2; quiz LR07x4 LR08x4 LR06x2 LR04 LR18 RC13 RC04x2 RC06 RC01 RC05.
// Full-length passage (p2): why zebras have stripes (the older camouflage, predator, heat and social ideas; the 2014 comparative study across zebras, horses and asses; field trials with horseflies and striped horse coats; the plains zebra temperature survey), natural science, written fresh.
// Weekdays computed 2026-10-16: Saturday, January 16; Monday, January 18 (Martin Luther King Jr. Day, a Colorado state holiday); Tuesday, January 19; Wednesday, January 20; Thursday, January 21; Sunday, January 24; Wednesday, January 27; Thursday, January 28, 2027. Saturday, October 27 and Sunday, October 28, 2007; Tuesday, June 3, 2008. June 12, 2027 is 21 weeks after January 16.
// Ronnie Galvan's claim (Luis swung first) lands here per the reveal schedule. Luis denies it on the January 20 call; on January 27 Dani doesn't make her move.
// Parole isn't mentioned in this chapter. Bible fix 10/16: no hearing yet, first eligibility about 2030.
// All questions, passages and answer choices are original. Not affiliated with or endorsed by LSAC.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 4, part: `Part One: The Argument`, title: `Weight`, weeks: `January 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR04`,`LR06`,`LR07`,`LR08`,`LR18`,`RC01`,`RC04`,`RC05`,`RC06`,`RC13`],
  cast: [
    [`Ronnie Galvan`,`Bartender at the Night Owl Tap in 2007, 49 now. Runs a taqueria in Commerce City. Told police that night that Luis swung first, and has never changed a word of it.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Keeps Gene's notebook on her desk in its freezer bag.`],
    [`Gene Vigil`,`Retired detective, 72. Gave Dani his 2007 notebook and said he'd swear to what's in it.`],
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, at Sterling. Calls Wednesdays at seven.`],
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 29. Fixes whatever breaks in the house they grew up in.`],
    [`Lupe Ruiz`,`Teo and Bianca's daughter, 6. Has a knight on her fridge from a grandfather she's never met.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Saturday, January 16. Gene Vigil's notebook lived on the corner of Ana's desk now, still in its freezer bag. Ana had read it three times that week, and after every reading she zipped the bag shut, the whole length of the seal, slowly, as if Gene could see her from Northglenn.`,
    `Twenty one weeks to June. Dani had started timing herself after New Year's: one full Logical Reasoning section on Sunday mornings, thirty five minutes on her phone, at the kitchen table under the photo on the fridge. Last Sunday she'd finished with four minutes to spare and missed six. Ana had asked to see the six. Ana always asked to see the wrong ones.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"We're going to file in March,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It goes to the DA in Brighton, and it lands on Meredith Sloane's desk. Chief deputy. She's the best they have, and she's fair, which is worse. She'll read every line we write looking for the soft spot."</span> She tapped the pencil on the freezer bag. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So before she does, we weigh things."</span>`,
    `Phil's box was open on the floor. Ana had pulled three sheets out of it and laid them side by side: a handwritten statement taken the night of the fight, a page from the March 2008 hearing, and a page from the trial. The same name on all three. Ronald Galvan.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Everybody in this case saw a jacket,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Consuelo saw a jacket. You saw a jacket. The lab saw blood on a jacket. Ronnie Galvan is the only witness who ever saw your father do anything."</span>`,
    `Dani knew what he'd said. She'd read it in the transcript years ago, and she'd decided then what to do with it. Pa said everybody was shoving. A bartender pouring four deep for a bar full of Boston fans saw what he saw. She'd put it in a drawer in her head and shut the drawer.`,
    `Dicta got up off the rug, turned around once, and lay back down facing the other way, which was as much as she ever said about anything.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: weight`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The scale`,
        body: [`January on Federal, and the laundromat's windows are fogged to the sills. Dicta goes to the vet in the morning, so tonight she gets the whole rug and no argument from me. Pencil.`,
          `Last time you learned to find where an argument breaks. Tonight the argument stays whatever it is, and you change how much it weighs. A strengthen question hands you an argument and five new facts and asks which one, if true, gives the conclusion the most support. A weaken question asks which one takes the most away. Same skill, two directions.`,
          `Two words in the stem do most of the work. "If true" means you don't argue with the choice. Accept it, however strange it sounds, and ask what it does to the conclusion. "Most" means you're ranking. Several choices may push a little. You want the one that pushes hardest on the spot where the argument actually needs help.`,
          `The right answer doesn't have to prove anything, or disprove anything. It has to move the needle. People skip the right answer all the time because it doesn't settle the case. It was never supposed to settle the case.`,
          `So the order hasn't changed. Conclusion first. Then the premises. Then the gap between them, said out loud in one sentence. A strengthener closes that gap a little. A weakener pries it open. If you can't say the gap, you can't tell which choices touch it.`],
        terms: [[`Strengthen`,`A new fact that, if true, makes the conclusion more likely to follow from the premises.`],[`Weaken`,`A new fact that, if true, makes the conclusion less likely to follow, without denying the premises.`]],
        exam: `Strengthen stems ask which choice, if true, most strengthens or most supports the argument. Weaken stems ask which one most weakens it, most seriously undermines it, or casts the most doubt on it. Treat every choice as true and find the one that moves the conclusion furthest.` },
      { h: `Where to push`,
        body: [`Most of these arguments have one of a few gaps, and each gap tells you where the weight goes.`,
          `Cause. Two things go together, so one caused the other. A weakener offers another cause that explains both, or shows the effect came first, or shows the effect turning up where the cause wasn't. A strengthener rules out a rival cause, or shows that more of the cause brought more of the effect. Best of all, it shows what happened when somebody changed the cause on purpose and held everything else still.`,
          `A sample. What's true of the people who were asked is true of everybody. A weakener shows the people asked were different from everybody in a way that matters. A strengthener shows they weren't.`,
          `A fit. The evidence fits one explanation, so that's the explanation. You know this one from last time. A weakener names another explanation the evidence fits just as well. A strengthener closes one of those off.`,
          `Now the traps. The out of scope choice is about the topic and not about the gap, and it's the wrong answer you'll meet most. The reversed choice does the job the stem didn't ask for: it weakens when you were told to strengthen. Read the stem twice, because near the end of a section your hand will pick a weakener for a strengthen question and feel fine about it. The echo choice just repeats a premise, sometimes with more people saying it, and adds nothing the argument didn't already have.`,
          `One difference from the assumption questions. On a necessary assumption question, a choice that claims too much is a trap. Here it isn't. If a choice is strong and it pushes the right way, strong is good.`],
        terms: [[`Alternative cause`,`Something other than the cause the argument names that could produce the same effect.`],[`Reversed answer`,`A choice that strengthens when the stem asks you to weaken, or weakens when it asks you to strengthen.`],[`Out of scope`,`A choice about the argument's topic that doesn't touch the step from premises to conclusion.`]],
        exam: `Expect at least one choice that does the opposite of what the stem asks. Before you confirm an answer, say the stem's verb back to yourself, strengthen or weaken, and check that your choice does that one.` },
      { h: `Weight both ways`,
        body: [`Here's what the prep books leave out. Real evidence pushes on more than one conclusion at once. A witness can hurt you on one question and help you on the next, and you don't get to keep only the half you like.`,
          `Say a neighbor saw a man slam a car door, and saw that the car was blue. Both facts came out of the same mouth. If you believe her about the door, you'd better believe her about the blue, and the other side has to live with that too. Weigh each fact against the conclusion it actually touches, and keep them apart.`,
          `Two more things about weight. A story told the same way three times by one person is one source, told three times. Consistency counts for something. It isn't the same as a second person, or a piece of paper, saying the same thing. Support from somewhere independent weighs more than repetition.`,
          `And a weakener isn't a disproof, any more than a strengthener is a proof. Knock out one support and the conclusion may still be true. You've shown it stands on less. That's all you've shown, and on this test that's plenty.`],
        terms: [[`Independent corroboration`,`Support for a claim from a separate source, which weighs more than one source repeating itself.`]],
        exam: `Weaken choices don't contradict the premises, because the test treats those as true. The right one adds something new that makes the step to the conclusion less safe. If a choice seems to deny a premise outright, read it again.` },
      { h: `Passages push too`,
        body: [`Reading Comprehension asks the same thing about a passage. Which of these, if true, would most strengthen the author's claim, or weaken a view the passage describes? Pin down which claim first, and whose. A passage often holds several views, and the question picks one. A fact that helps the author can hurt a critic the author quotes.`,
          `Then do what you'd do in Logical Reasoning. Find the support the passage gives for that claim, find the gap, and pick the fact that works on the gap.`,
          `The other question tonight asks what somebody would agree with: the author, or a person the passage describes. Stay close to the page. The right answer is something that person says, or something that follows from what they say with almost no stretch. The traps are things they might well believe and never say, things that go further than they went, and things somebody else in the passage said. Keep the voices apart. Put a name in the margin next to each view the first time you meet it.`,
          `Agree answers tend to be mild. If a choice says always or never or proves, find the line that says it. Usually there isn't one.`],
        terms: [[`Would agree with`,`A claim the author, or a person the passage describes, holds: stated, or very nearly stated, in the text.`]],
        exam: `Agree stems ask which statement the author, or a named person, would be most likely to agree with. Strengthen and weaken stems in Reading Comprehension name the claim; underline it before you read the choices.` }
    ],
    video: [`Xei7-RN-6Ig`,`LSAT Strengthen and Weaken Made Easy`,`LSAT JP`,`9:14`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f4s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR07`, title:`The cap`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The whiteboard hadn't changed since December. The long sentence across the top, the line under it, the four lines under that. Line two, SOME OF THE BLOOD CAME FROM THE ATTACK, NOT THE BAR. Line four, NEAR THE LOT AT 11:15. Off to the side, nowhere near any of them, CAP and BOY?`,
        `Ana wrote under CAP, in pencil this time, not marker. THE MAN WORE A CAP. LUIS NEVER WORE ONE. SO THE MAN WASN'T LUIS.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's ours,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It isn't proof, and it doesn't have to be. It's weight. Give me one fact that, if it turned out true, would put the most weight on it."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That everybody who knew him, at work and at home, says he never once wore a hat."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the premise with more people saying it,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Habits have exceptions. Meredith won't ask about every night. She'll ask about one. Close the one night."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Partial. It adds witnesses to a premise the argument already grants. The gap is that particular night: a man who never wears a hat can wear one once. The best strengthener works on that.`},
        {t:`"That whoever saw him walk out of the bar at 10:45 saw him with nothing on his head."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes ASK GALVAN on the board, and underlines it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Half an hour before. It doesn't close the gap all the way. A man can find a cap in thirty minutes. But now they need a cap from nowhere, and that's their problem, not ours."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. The argument's weak point is the night itself. Seeing him bareheaded thirty minutes before the attack narrows the gap: for the man in the cap to be Luis, he'd have had to put one on between the bar and the curb. It doesn't prove anything. It doesn't have to.`},
        {t:`"That the refinery gave every man on the crew a red company cap to go with his red jacket."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana's pencil stops. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then he owned one. Then the cap fits him exactly as well as the jacket did. You just handed Meredith a hat."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Reversed. If the crew had matching caps, the cap points toward him instead of away. A strengthener has to push the conclusion your way.`},
        {t:`"That Phil Abrams never asked Consuelo Baca one question about a cap at trial."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"True, and sad, and it doesn't touch who was under the cap,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's an argument about Phil. We're not arguing about Phil."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Out of scope. What the defense lawyer failed to ask says nothing about whether the man in the cap was Luis.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s2`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, title:`Meredith`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana took off her reading glasses and set them on Phil's box, folded. Dani had seen her do that twice: once to play a judge, and once, on the phone, to play her own landlord, who wanted the rent and a reason. Somebody else was coming into the room.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Five minutes. I'm Meredith Sloane."</span> Even her chair creaked different. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I've read your cap. I've read your dispatch log, which your side had in 2008 and sat on, thanks. I've got a jury's verdict and a woman across the street who saw red. So go on. What do I say that does your cap the most damage?"</span>`,
        `Dani had thought like a prosecutor for eight years, in other people's cases. Never in this one. It took her a second to find the switch.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That a man who means to hurt somebody might put on a hat he'd never wear, so nobody knows him."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana, as Meredith, dry as toast: <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Mr. Ruiz never wore hats, Your Honor. That's exactly why he wore one."</span> Glasses back on. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's a lousy argument,"</span> Ana says, herself again. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's also her best, because it doesn't need Consuelo wrong about anything. She borrows our fact and turns it around."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. The strongest attack gives the cap an explanation that fits Luis: a man hiding who he is wears what he never wears. And it leaves Consuelo's account alone, which matters, since the State needs her eyes for the red.`},
        {t:`"That Consuelo was sixty four and looking across a dark street, and could've been wrong about a cap."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She won't touch it,"</span> Ana says, in her own voice again. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Call Consuelo's eyes bad and the jacket goes out the window right behind the cap. Meredith needs those eyes. She needs them for the red."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. It would hurt the cap, sure. It would hurt the State's own identification just as badly. When the question is what one particular person would argue, count what else the weakener breaks.`},
        {t:`"That nobody said a single word about a cap at the trial, and the jury convicted him anyway."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That one's ours,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A jury can't weigh what it never heard. Meredith won't bring that up. I will."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. It doesn't weaken anything. The jury never hearing about the cap is part of the motion's case, and it says nothing against what the cap shows.`},
        {t:`"That the bartender who watched him leave that night says he walked out with no hat on."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana snorts. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Meredith would pay you good money not to say that. If it's true, it's ours. Go find out if it's true."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. Reversed. A sighting of him bareheaded would help the cap argument, not hurt it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC04`, title:`Four blocks`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, January 18, the office was closed for the holiday, and at two thirty Dani was in a booth in a strip mall off Highway 2, four blocks from the vape shop that used to be the Night Owl. The lunch rush was over. A radio in the kitchen, a woman wiping tables, a man's voice back there laughing at something.`,
        `She ordered and didn't eat it. She took out the copy Ana had made of the first sheet, the one from the night itself.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Commerce City Police Department, witness statement of Ronald Galvan, taken by Officer D. Archuleta, Saturday, October 27, 2007, 2340 hours">I'm the bartender at the Night Owl. I was working the bar alone after the game. It was packed, mostly Boston people and the refinery guys. Around 10:25 I heard yelling over by the hallway to the bathrooms and I looked up. Luis Ruiz, who comes in Saturdays with the Platte Valley crew, swung at a guy I didn't know and hit him in the face. Luis swung first. The other guy came back at him and they were grabbing and shoving. I came over the bar and got between them. The other guy had beer all down his front. Luis's nose was bleeding on his jacket. I told Luis to go home and cool off, and he went out the back about 10:45. I didn't see the other guy go. I never had trouble with Luis before. I don't know what it was about.</prop>`,
        `She'd read it before. She read it now as Ana had taught her to read anything a witness wrote: only what he'd stand behind, and nothing she wanted to add.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`He saw who threw the first punch, and he can't say what led up to it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Two sentences, and they don't fight each other. Luis swung first. I don't know what it was about. He put both down on the same page, twenty minutes before midnight, and he never tried to make one of them bigger than the other.`,
          why:`Would agree with. Both halves are in the statement: "Luis swung first" and "I don't know what it was about," along with "I looked up" at the yelling. He vouches for the punch and nothing before it.`},
        {t:`Luis had never caused him trouble, so the other man must have provoked him.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The first half is his. "I never had trouble with Luis before." The second half is hers. He doesn't say provoked. He says he doesn't know.`,
          why:`Would agree with. Half supported. He does say he never had trouble with Luis. He never draws a conclusion about provocation, and he says outright he doesn't know what the fight was about.`},
        {t:`Luis came back inside later that night to look for the man he'd fought.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She looks for it. Out the back about 10:45. I didn't see the other guy go. Nobody comes back inside anywhere on this page.`,
          why:`Would agree with. Not in the statement. He describes Luis leaving and says nothing about him returning.`},
        {t:`Several of the refinery men in the bar joined the fight on Luis's side.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The refinery guys are in the first lines, filling the bar. In the fight there are two men and one bartender. She'd moved a crowd into a fight it never touched.`,
          why:`Would agree with. Contradicted. The statement mentions refinery men only as customers. The fight he describes is between two men, and he breaks it up alone.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s4`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, title:`Four deep`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The man in the kitchen came out wiping his hands on a towel over his shoulder. Heavy in the forearms, gray at the temples, reading glasses pushed up on his head. He saw her and stopped walking.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"You're Luis's girl,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"You've got his whole face."</span> He sat down across from her without asking, the towel still on his shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"He used to carry your report cards in his wallet. Showed them to the whole bar. You got a B in chemistry once and he wanted to go down to that school and have words with somebody."</span>`,
        `She hadn't known that. She didn't know what to do with her face.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I know why you're here."</span> He folded his hands on the table. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I'll tell you what I told everybody. I was pulling a pitcher. I heard yelling and I looked up and Luis hit the guy, right in the face. Your dad swung first, Dani. I'm sorry. I'd give a lot for it to be different."</span>`,
        `She'd come in with one question, about a hat. She found she had another one first.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"You've had nineteen years. Nobody remembers a bar fight that clearly after that long."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I told the cop that night,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Before anybody knew the guy would die. It's in his handwriting."</span> It's on the table between them. She'd aimed at his memory, and his memory was written down before it had time to go anywhere.`,
          why:`Weaken. Memory does fade, so this has some force in general. But his account was recorded the same night. Attacking a memory that was written down at the time aims at the wrong target.`},
        {t:`"You worked for that bar. You needed those cops happy, or you'd lose the liquor license."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Something closes in his face. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I poured for the owner. I didn't own a thing. I didn't need those cops for anything."</span> He's quiet a moment. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I liked your dad more than I liked any of them."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. It goes after his motive instead of his account, and a witness with a motive can still be right. Here the motive doesn't even fit the facts.`},
        {t:`"You looked up at the yelling. Whatever came before the yelling, you never saw."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`He thinks about it. Doesn't look away. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"That's true. I didn't see what started it. I saw who swung."</span> He turns his coffee cup a little. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Something started it. Luis didn't just hit people."</span> It's a dent. It's in the part she didn't come for.`,
          why:`Weaken. Read what he claims: Luis swung first. Then read where he was looking. He looked up at the yelling, so anything before the yelling, a grab, a shove, somebody else's fist, he missed. "First" turns into "first that I saw." He's still good on the punch. He was never good on why.`},
        {t:`"You've told it the same way every time. To the officer, at the hearing, at the trial."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"That's right,"</span> he says, and nods, because she's just made his case for him. Three tellings, one story. She meant it as a question.`,
          why:`Weaken. Reversed. Telling it the same way three times makes his account more credible, not less.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s5`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR07`, title:`Before anybody knew`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Once he'd started he kept going. It was a night he'd told too many times and never once all the way to the end.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"The guy who died. He'd been on the Boston people since the third inning. Loud. I almost cut him off twice. When the yelling started he had his back to me, bent over some, yelling down at something by the hallway. I couldn't see over the people. He was already wet. Beer all down him. Somebody'd knocked a glass into him, I figured."</span>`,
        `Then she asked hers. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"When Pa went out the back. Was he wearing a hat?"</span>`,
        `Ronnie actually smiled. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Luis? Luis never wore a hat in his life. Said they made him look like his brother."</span> The smile went. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"No hat. I'd swear to it. Nobody ever asked me that."</span>`,
        `She asked about a bar tab. Phil had wanted one in 2008. Ronnie shook his head. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"Cash bar on a Series night. Nobody ran a tab. I couldn't tell you half the names in there."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"I wrote him once,"</span> he said, to his coffee. <span class="said" data-who="Ronnie">"After the trial. He never wrote back. I don't blame him."</span>`,
        `She believed him. About the hat, and about the punch. That was the trouble with believing somebody. You didn't get to pick.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That he told the officer at twenty to twelve, before anybody knew it was a homicide.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`2340 hours, the statement says. Travis Hensley died at 1:40. At twenty to twelve there was no murder yet, only a fight and a man on a stretcher. Ronnie said swung first before anybody knew what story they'd need.`,
          why:`Strengthen. An account given before there was any case to fit it to can't have been shaped by the case. The timing rules out the most likely way an honest witness goes wrong: telling the story the investigation later needs.`},
        {t:`That he still remembers it as clearly now as he did when he testified in 2008.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Clear isn't the same as right. People are surest about the stories they've told most. It helps a little that he hasn't drifted. It's still one man, remembering.`,
          why:`Strengthen. A little. Stable memory is better than shifting memory, but confidence and clarity don't show accuracy. The strongest support comes from when the account was given.`},
        {t:`That the taqueria has done well and he's never been in any trouble since.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`She's glad for him. It has nothing to do with 10:25 on a Saturday in 2007. Good men see things wrong too.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Out of scope. His later life says nothing about whether he saw the first punch correctly.`},
        {t:`That the officer who wrote it down was a rookie who'd never taken a statement.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's even true. Gene had said rookie. But Gene had also written, the next morning, same as he gave Archuleta. Either way it's a reason to doubt the page, and she'd come looking for support.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Reversed. An inexperienced officer recording the statement would weaken confidence in it, not strengthen it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s6`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC13`, title:`Sunday noon`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`That night she brushed her teeth at the bathroom sink, the same sink, the same mirror with the silver going at one corner. She'd read grabbing and shoving at the taqueria, and it waited for the mirror, nineteen years deep.`,
        `Sunday, October 28, 2007. She'd come home at eight and gone to sleep on the couch. Around noon she'd gotten up to pee, and the bathroom door was open, and her father was shaving. He never shaved on a Sunday. Never. A square of toilet paper was stuck to his neck under his jaw, the left side, with a red dot coming through it. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Cut myself, mija. Go back to sleep."</span>`,
        `She'd gone back to sleep. Gene came at three. By then it was a nick under his collar, and nobody looks twice at a man who's shaved. He never shaved on Sundays. She'd never once asked herself why he had that one. She'd never told a soul, because there had never been anything to tell.`,
        `She sat on the edge of the tub with Ronnie's statement on her knees. The other guy came back at him and they were grabbing and shoving. She went through what she had, one thing at a time, and asked which of them did the most for that sentence.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That Ronnie has told it the same way three times, in nearly the same words.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's worth something that he hasn't drifted. But if he got it wrong at twenty to twelve, he's been getting it wrong ever since, just as steadily. She wants something that didn't start in his mouth.`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Consistency adds a little, but it's the same witness repeating himself. Independent support for the grabbing would weigh far more.`},
        {t:`That several men from the refinery wore the same red jacket in the bar that night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`True, and it's the best thing she has about the lot. It says nothing at all about who had his hands on whom inside.`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Out of scope. The number of red jackets bears on identification in the lot, not on whether the two men grappled in the bar.`},
        {t:`That on Sunday Pa was shaving at noon with a scrap of tissue on his neck.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`It could have been a razor. She knows that. But it's the only thing on her list that didn't come out of Ronnie's mouth, and it sits right where a man's hands go when he's grabbing somebody. She doesn't write it down. She doesn't need to.`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. The statement says the men were grabbing and shoving. A fresh mark on Luis's neck the next morning is independent support for that, from a different source. It fits a shaving cut too, which is why it strengthens rather than proves.`},
        {t:`That Pa told Gene on Sunday afternoon he never touched anybody in that bar.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`That's the other side of the scale. If Pa's right, Ronnie's wrong. It can't help Ronnie's sentence. It's the sentence Ronnie's is fighting.`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Reversed. Luis's denial contradicts the statement's account, so it weakens the claim instead of strengthening it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s7`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC13`, title:`Put it together`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, January 19, she told Ana about the hat. She told her about the punch, and about the report cards. She didn't tell her about the bathroom.`,
        `Ana listened without writing anything down. When Dani got to "nobody ever asked me that," Ana said, very quietly, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"No. Nobody did."</span>`,
        (G.usedthesystem
          ?`Then she pulled the 2008 closing out of Phil's binder, not the copy Dani had once printed at work. Dani noticed which one she chose. She read the last lines aloud.`
          :`Then she pulled the 2008 closing out of Phil's binder and read the last lines aloud.`),
        `<prop type="doc" title="Trial transcript, Monday, June 9, 2008, the prosecution's closing argument, excerpt">What she saw was a man in a red jacket strike Travis Hensley at a quarter past eleven and run. So who wore a red jacket that night? The defendant did. Put it together, and the man in the red jacket was Luis Ruiz.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Put it together,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the whole closing in three words. Of everything new since June of 2008, what hurts it most?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ronnie saw Pa go out bareheaded, and the man Consuelo saw had a ball cap on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the one,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not because it proves he wasn't there. Because she told them to put it together, and now there's a piece that doesn't go."</span> She writes BAREHEADED 10:45, GALVAN under CAP, and dates it.`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. The closing claims the pieces add up to Luis. A cap on the attacker, set beside a sighting of Luis bareheaded thirty minutes earlier, is a piece that points the other way, and the jury heard neither half.`},
        {t:`Ronnie told the police that night that a lot of the crew wore the same red jacket.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It hurts it,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It isn't new. It's in the first report anybody wrote, and Phil had it in June. I asked what's new."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. It weakens the jacket inference, but it was in the record at trial, so it can't be the new fact the question asks for. Read every word of the stem.`},
        {t:`Ronnie says Pa threw the first punch, and he's never once changed a word of it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's theirs,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the one thing in the closing they had right. And it isn't new either."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Reversed. Ronnie's account of the punch supports the State's story about the fight. It doesn't weaken anything.`},
        {t:`Ronnie says Travis was already soaked in beer when the yelling first started.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Ana writes it down anyway, small, in the corner. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Interesting. It doesn't touch the jacket or the lot. File it."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. Out of scope. The beer bears on how the fight began, not on whether the man in the lot was Luis.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s8`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC04`, title:`Page four`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana unzipped the freezer bag, slowly, and took out the notebook. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I read this page Thursday and didn't know what it was for."</span> She found it without hunting and turned it around.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Gene Vigil's notebook, Sunday, October 28, 2007, page 4">1015. R. Galvan, 30. Bartender, Night Owl. At his apt, just woke up.<br>Same as he gave Archuleta. Ruiz swung first, approx 2225. Hit V in face.<br>Didn't see what started it. Looked up at yelling.<br>V had beer down front.<br>Knows Ruiz 6 or 7 yrs. Likes him. Upset telling it.<br>Good wit. If he's shading it, it's toward Ruiz.<br>Ruiz out the back approx 2245.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Gene had it Sunday morning,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Didn't see what started it. Five hours before he sat down with your father."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Forget what you'd say about Ronnie. What would Gene say, from this page?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That Ronnie was a good witness, so anything he said about Luis was to be trusted.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Good wit,"</span> Ana reads. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He'd sign the first half. Not anything. Gene writes down what Ronnie didn't see, right there on the same page."</span>`,
          why:`Would agree with. Half right. Gene calls him a good witness, but he also records a limit, that Ronnie didn't see what started it. "Anything he said" goes further than the page.`},
        {t:`That what started the fight mattered more than who happened to throw the very first punch.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He wrote it down and never went back to it,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's what a man does with something he doesn't think matters."</span>`,
          why:`Would agree with. Not supported. Gene notes the gap and moves on. Nothing on the page suggests he thought the cause of the fight outweighed the punch.`},
        {t:`That a witness who likes the man he's describing is more believable when it hurts him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"If he's shading it, it's toward Ruiz,"</span> Ana reads. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Gene believed Ronnie because Ronnie didn't want it to be true. So do I, for what it's worth. So did I in 2010."</span>`,
          why:`Would agree with. "Likes him. Upset telling it," and then, "If he's shading it, it's toward Ruiz." Gene reasons that a witness who's sorry to say a thing is more credible for saying it. That's on the page, very nearly stated.`},
        {t:`That Ronnie was too upset that morning for anything he said to be relied on at all.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He wrote upset,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then he wrote good wit. He thought one was the reason for the other."</span>`,
          why:`Would agree with. Contradicted. Gene notes that Ronnie was upset and still calls him a good witness. He treats the distress as a sign of honesty, not unreliability.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, title:`Everybody was pushing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[((s.flags.calledgenealiar||G.calledgenealiar)
          ?`Tuesday night, on the drive home, her phone had lit up with a Northglenn number. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"This is Vigil."</span> Business, the whole call. Brennan's letter had come. He'd swear to the notebook any day she named. Then, after a pause she could hear him decide about: <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Galvan called me Monday night. First time in years. You saw him."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Monday."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"He threw up in the courthouse lot after he testified. Behind my car. I held his coffee."</span> Another pause. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I drove the Buick to church Sunday. First time."</span> He hung up before she could say anything to that.`
          :`Tuesday night, on the drive home, her phone had lit up with a Northglenn number. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"It's Gene."</span> Brennan's letter had come. He'd swear to the notebook any day she named. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"Galvan called me Monday night. First time in years. You saw him."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Monday."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"He threw up in the courthouse lot after he testified. Behind my car. I held his coffee."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Gene">"I drove the Buick to church Sunday. First time. She'd have laughed at me, how slow I took the turns."</span>`),
        `Wednesday, January 20, seven. On her way down she stepped off the bottom stair into cold water, through her sock. A dark tongue of it ran out from under the washer across the concrete. She sat two steps up, wet foot and all, as the recording started.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Bishop out,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Right up next to your knight."</span> Then: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Furnace holding?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Teo fixed it in October."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I know who fixed it."</span>`,
        `She told him she'd been to see Ronnie Galvan. The hiss. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Ronnie. He still pouring?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"He's got a taqueria."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Good for him. He always wanted that."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Pa. He says you swung first."</span>`,
        `It came back fast, like it had been waiting at the front of his mouth for nineteen years. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Ronnie was pouring four deep. Everybody was pushing. It was a World Series. I never hit that man, mija. I never touched him."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Ronnie's told it the same way for nineteen years now, Pa. Why would he make it up?"`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I didn't say he made it up. I said he was pouring."</span> He has an answer for Ronnie. He's had nineteen years to find one. Ronnie on one side, Pa on the other, and nothing on the table between them but her.`,
          why:`Weaken. It leans on Ronnie's credibility, which is real, but Luis has a ready answer, a busy bartender's view. When it's one person's word against another's, the stronger weakener is evidence that doesn't depend on whom you believe.`},
        {t:`"Then you lied to Gene about everything, didn't you? About that whole night."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Don't,"</span> he says. Not loud. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Don't put everything in one sentence, mija. You never used to."</span> She had a point, and she buried it under a bigger one he could refuse.`,
          why:`Weaken. Overreach. Even if he lied about the punch, that doesn't show he lied about everything. A weakener aimed at the whole night is easier to brush off than one aimed at the claim in front of you.`},
        {t:`"You were always the gentlest man I knew, Pa. You never hit anybody."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"That's right,"</span> he says, too quickly, and she hears herself handing him back his own answer. She'd come down the stairs to test it.`,
          why:`Weaken. Reversed. His character, as she remembers it, supports his denial instead of weakening it.`},
        {t:`"Pa, his blood was on your cuff. If you never touched him, how did it get there?"`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`The hiss goes on. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Everybody was bleeding on everybody,"</span> he says finally. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"It was a bar."</span> It's an answer that gives up more than it says. If everybody was bleeding on everybody, there was a fight. She doesn't say so. She hears him hear it.`,
          why:`Weaken. Physical evidence is the hardest thing for "I never touched him" to absorb. Travis's blood on his sleeve needs contact to get there, and the denial leaves no room for contact. It doesn't show what happened in the lot. It shows the denial can't be right as he states it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR08`, title:`The guy`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He'd made her give him her move before they hung up. A pawn, pushed up at his bishop. She'd moved it on her own board with her wet foot on the step and hadn't felt it. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I still have her two pages,"</span> he'd said at the end, like it had just come to him. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Brennan's. I keep them with your mother's letters."</span> Then the minute was up.`,
        `Thursday, January 21, two tamales sat on a paper plate on Dani's side of Ana's desk. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The woman downstairs makes them Thursdays. I bought four. I don't eat four."</span> She didn't look up from what she was writing. Dani ate both.`,
        `Line three on the board. 3. "THE GUY FROM THE BAR" WAS LUIS. Ana put the cap back on the marker and tapped the line with it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Shannon's phone call. Their reading is that 'the guy' meant your father. What hurts that reading most?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Ronnie says Travis spent the whole game picking at Boston people. 'The guy' could be any of them."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes MOUTHING OFF SINCE THE 3RD next to line three. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Their reading needs one guy. Now it's a bar full of guys with a reason. That's the sentence I want Meredith to have to answer."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. The State reads "the guy from the bar" as the man Travis fought because he was the only man Travis had trouble with. Evidence that Travis had trouble with others gives the phrase other men to point at. That's exactly the weak point.`},
        {t:`"It was a World Series night. That bar was packed with men from the first pitch right to the last out."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Packed with men who never spoke to him,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A crowd isn't a suspect. Give me men he had a reason to call 'the guy.'"</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. It points the right way, but a crowd alone doesn't give Travis a reason to mean any one of them. The stronger weakener names other men he'd had trouble with.`},
        {t:`"Shannon says he sounded embarrassed on the phone, not scared, when he asked her to come."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's about him,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not about who he meant. Embarrassed men still mean somebody."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. Out of scope. How Travis sounded says nothing about whom "the guy" referred to.`},
        {t:`"Ronnie watched Pa walk out the back door at a quarter to eleven, right toward the lot."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's theirs,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Out the back is into the lot. You just put him where they want him."</span>`,
          why:`Weaken. Reversed. Luis heading out toward the lot fits the State's reading of the call. It strengthens it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR07`, title:`Something with a clock`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana drew the timeline along the bottom edge of the board in pencil, in handwriting so small Dani had to stand up to read it. 10:25 FIGHT. 10:45 OUT THE BACK. 11:04 CALL. 11:15 ATTACK. 11:17 911.`,
        `Between the 10:45 and the 11:04 she wrote GONE BEFORE 11:04? Then she stood with the pencil an inch off the board, and Dicta, who could always tell when nobody was moving, lifted her head to check.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's what we'd like to say. Right now it's only what he says, and what he says proves what he says. One fact. If it walked in that door tomorrow, which one helps that line most?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That he told Gene on Sunday the same 10:45 he's told me every Wednesday since."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He's consistent,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So's Ronnie. So's the weather in Sterling. Consistent isn't a clock, and 10:45 is when he walked out of a bar. I asked about 11:04."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. A little. A story he hasn't changed is better than one he has. But it's still his word, and it's about leaving the bar, which nobody disputes. It doesn't place him anywhere at 11:04.`},
        {t:`"That his truck was still parked behind the bar when Travis made that 11:04 call."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then he was still there,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Or his truck was, which a jury hears as the same thing. You just did Meredith's homework for her."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Reversed. A truck still in the lot at 11:04 suggests he hadn't gone, which weakens the timeline.`},
        {t:`"Anything with a clock on it that had him somewhere else before 11:04. A receipt. A tape."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's my whole wish list in one sentence,"</span> Ana says. She writes RECORD? under the question mark and taps it twice. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody had one in 2008. Ask him where he went after that door. Not what he did. Where he went."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. A record with a time and a place does what testimony can't. It puts him somewhere else with nobody's memory in between. If it's near 11:04 and far enough from the lot, the call came while he was gone, and getting back to the curb by 11:15 gets hard. It doesn't prove he wasn't there. It makes the line on the board much safer.`},
        {t:`"That the last out of the game came at about 10:20, a few minutes before the fight."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Good for baseball,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's when the fight started. Nobody in this case is fighting about when the fight started."</span>`,
          why:`Strengthen. Out of scope. The end of the game fixes the time of the fight, which isn't in dispute. It does nothing for where Luis was after 10:45.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f4s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR07`, title:`On foot`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She'd texted Teo Thursday night. Basement floor's wet by the washer. He didn't answer. Sunday, January 24, at ten in the morning he was at the back door with a shop vac and his headlamp.`,
        `He lay on a flattened box with his head under the tub, swearing at it in a friendly way. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Tub seal. It's weeping out the bottom."</span> He slid out and lay there a second with the headlamp aimed at the joists. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"That's not a part, Dani. That's a whole washer."</span> She told him it worked fine. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"It works on your floor."</span> He sat up. The lamp swung across the shelf where the banker's box used to be. She'd carried it upstairs in December. He clicked it off. Not a word.`,
        `She told him about Ronnie over the vac, too loud. He shut it off mid sentence. Down there you could hear the furnace tick. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"The bartender,"</span> he said, and when she asked if he remembered him, <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Pa talked about him."</span> Nothing else. He wound the cord over his elbow and around his hand, over and under, every loop the same size. Then, still winding: <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Maybe Pa had a reason."</span> She said he couldn't know that. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You can't know he didn't."</span>`,
        `A minute later, mostly to the cord: <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Guy who did it ran, right? On foot."</span> Pa didn't walk to the mailbox, he said. Pa drove to the end of the block for milk and left it running. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"If he'd done something out there he'd have been in that truck and gone. That's all I'm saying."</span>`,
        `It was the most he'd said about that night in ten years, and he'd said it to a shop vac. She put it in Ana's order in her head, conclusion last: the man ran off on foot, Pa had his truck right there, men with trucks drive, so the man who ran probably wasn't Pa. She already knew Ana's next question. What would make that hold?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That Pa almost never walked anywhere he could drive. Not even to the end of the block."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I just said that,"</span> Teo says. He did, nearly word for word. It's the premise again, in her voice this time. And a scared man runs, truck or no truck.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Partial. It restates the habit the argument already relies on. The gap is whether a man with a truck that night might still have fled on foot, and this doesn't touch it.`},
        {t:`"That a lot of men who'd been drinking that night walked home instead of driving."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Teo frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Then he walks home. Then on foot's nothing."</span> He's right, and he doesn't look glad about it. She'd handed every drinking man in that bar a reason to be on foot, and their father said he'd had two.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Reversed. If drinkers commonly walked home, then fleeing on foot fits a man who'd driven there, and the argument loses its force.`},
        {t:`"That north of the lot, where he ran, there's no road for a truck. Just the ditch and the tracks."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Then he wasn't running to a truck,"</span> Teo says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"If that's what's out there."</span> He goes back to the cord. She writes DITCH? MAP on the back of her hand in pen, and he watches her do it, and doesn't ask.`,
          why:`Strengthen. The weak point is that a man might run on foot to a truck parked somewhere else. If there was nowhere north for a truck to be, the man wasn't running to one. That closes off the one route by which "on foot" could still fit a man who'd driven there.`},
        {t:`"That Consuelo called 911 less than two minutes after the man ran away from the curb."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"So?"</span> Teo says. Fair. Calling fast makes her a good neighbor. It doesn't put anybody in a truck or take him out of one.`,
          why:`Strengthen. Out of scope. How fast the call came in doesn't bear on whether the man who fled on foot had a vehicle.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f4s8`, title:`Strengthens, weakens, or neither`, intro:`Ana's index cards. Each card has an argument and one new fact. Take the fact as true. Does it strengthen the argument, weaken it, or do neither?`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. "The Elm Street bakery's sales doubled after it began opening at six instead of seven. So the earlier hours caused the jump." New fact: A rival bakery two blocks away closed for good the same week.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:1, why:`The rival's customers had to go somewhere. That's another cause for the jump, so the earlier hours explain less.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. "Students who used the new math app scored higher on the spring test than students who didn't. So the app improves scores." New fact: Teachers assigned the app to students at random.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:0, why:`Random assignment means the two groups started out alike, so the app becomes the best explanation for the difference.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. "The library's new evening hours drew more visitors, so the city should keep the branch open late." New fact: The library's front steps were last repainted in 2019.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:2, why:`Paint on the steps doesn't bear on whether the evening hours drew visitors or whether the city should keep them.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. "The witness says the car that hit the bike rack was green, so police should look for a green car." New fact: The witness was standing forty feet away, in daylight, with nothing in the way.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:0, why:`A clear, close, daylight view makes her color more likely to be right.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. "The crows stopped landing on the park's north lawn after the city installed sprinklers there. So the sprinklers keep them away." New fact: In the weeks the sprinklers were shut off for repairs, the crows came back.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:0, why:`Take the cause away and the effect goes with it. That's strong support for the sprinklers as the cause.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. "Since Dr. Ames began prescribing the new drug, fewer of her patients have gone back into the hospital. So the drug reduces readmissions." New fact: Over the same months, the hospital began calling every patient at home two days after discharge.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:1, why:`The calls are a rival cause that started at the same time. The drug may deserve less of the credit.`},
      {prompt:`Card seven. "The museum's free Thursday nights draw big crowds, so free admission brings in new visitors." New fact: The museum's gift shop sells more on Thursdays than on any other day.`, choices:[`Strengthens`,`Weakens`,`Neither`], answer:2, why:`Busy Thursdays would sell more gifts either way. The shop's sales don't say whether the crowds are new visitors.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Trial transcript, Tuesday, June 3, 2008, direct examination of Ronald Galvan`,
      text:[`Q. Mr. Galvan, where do you work?`,
        `A. The Night Owl Tap, on Highway 2. I tend bar.`,
        `Q. Were you working on Saturday, October 27, 2007?`,
        `A. Yes. By myself, after the game.`,
        `Q. Do you know the defendant?`,
        `A. Luis. He's come in Saturdays for years, with the guys from the refinery.`,
        `Q. What did you see at about 10:25 that night?`,
        `A. I heard yelling and looked up, and Luis hit a man in the face. The man who died. Then they were grabbing and shoving, and I went over the bar and got between them.`,
        `Q. Who threw the first punch?`,
        `A. Luis did.`,
        `Q. Are you sure?`,
        `A. I'm sure. I wish I wasn't.`,
        `Q. What happened next?`,
        `A. I told Luis to go home and cool off. He went out the back.`,
        `Q. What time was that?`,
        `A. About a quarter to eleven.`,
        `Q. What was the defendant wearing?`,
        `A. His work jacket. The red one. A lot of them had it on that night.`,
        `Q. Thank you. Nothing further.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: the stripes`,
      text:[`Ask why a zebra has stripes and you will get an answer, usually a confident one. Naturalists have been supplying them for well over a century. The stripes hide the animal in tall grass. They dazzle a lion trying to pick one zebra out of a running herd. They help it shed heat. They let zebras tell each other apart. Every one of these is plausible, and every one comes with a good story attached, which for a long time was exactly the trouble. A story that fits the stripes doesn't show that the stripes are there because of it, and for decades almost nobody set the stories against one another.`,
        `The first broad test looked at geography. In 2014 a team of biologists rated how heavily striped each species and population of zebras, horses, and wild asses is, then checked those ratings against several maps at once: where large predators range, how hot it gets, how much woodland there is, and where biting flies such as horseflies and tsetse flies stay active for much of the year. The stripes followed the flies. The predator maps matched poorly, which makes sense once you recall that lions eat plenty of animals with no stripes at all. A correlation isn't a test of cause, but this one gave a single hypothesis a lead it had never had.`,
        `Then came fieldwork aimed at how it might work. Researchers who filmed horseflies around zebras and around plain coated horses saw the flies approach both. On the horses, the flies slowed and landed. On the zebras, they often failed to slow down at all, and bumped into the animal or overshot it. In one experiment, the same horses wore striped coats and then plain ones. In stripes they drew far fewer landings, while their uncovered heads were landed on about as often as before. Same horses, same smell, same habits: the difference lay in the pattern itself. That matters, because biting flies carry diseases that can kill horses and zebras, and a zebra's short coat makes it an easy target.`,
        `Nobody should call the case closed. A survey of plains zebra populations found the boldest stripes in the hottest places, and some researchers think heat plays a part after all. Others answer that hot places are also where biting flies do best, so the two patterns are hard to untangle. When experimenters have measured striped and plain surfaces side by side in the sun, they have found little or no cooling from the stripes.`,
        `What may last longest is a lesson about method. The older explanations weren't knocked down one at a time. They were outgrown, once somebody asked what each idea predicted about the world and went to look. An explanation that merely fits the animal costs nothing. One that says which animals will be striped, and where, can turn out wrong, and that is exactly what makes it worth believing when it doesn't.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Ecologist: Along the Mill River, tree swallow pairs nesting in boxes have raised more chicks per nest in years when the river's spring mayfly hatch was large than in years when it was small. So the size of the mayfly hatch affects how many chicks the swallows can raise.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the ecologist's argument?`,
      opts:[`When researchers added insects at randomly chosen boxes, those boxes raised more chicks.`,`Tree swallows on the Mill River also eat many insects other than mayflies during the nesting season.`,`Large mayfly hatches on the Mill River tend to come in springs that are warmer than usual.`,`The number of nest boxes along the Mill River has grown steadily over the past twenty years.`,`Tree swallows prefer nest boxes near water to boxes put up in open fields away from any river.`],
      a:0, why:`Strengthen. The argument infers a cause from a pattern across years. Adding food at randomly chosen boxes tests the cause directly, with the year held fixed: more insects, more chicks. Other insects in the diet don't connect hatch size to chicks. Warm springs are a rival cause, which weakens. The number of boxes doesn't touch chicks per nest, and a preference for water says nothing about food.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Archivist: The letter in our collection signed by the diarist Mary Kerr is dated March 1851, and for decades it was cataloged under that year. But it can't have been written then. It mentions the Linton and Harrow Railroad by name, and that line wasn't finished until 1856.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the archivist's argument?`,
      opts:[`Kerr is known to have written many letters about her travels during the 1850s.`,`The other letters in the collection signed by Kerr are all correctly dated.`,`The letter describes a trip Kerr took on the line from one end to the other.`,`Plans for the Linton and Harrow line were printed in newspapers in 1849.`,`Kerr lived most of her life near the route the line would eventually take.`],
      a:2, why:`Strengthen. The gap: a letter can mention a railroad before it's finished, as a plan. If the letter describes riding the whole line, the line had to exist, and an 1851 date fails. Published plans in 1849 open the "plan" explanation, which weakens. Her other travel letters, the dating of other letters, and where she lived don't close the gap.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Office manager: Last spring we began letting employees set their own hours, as long as everyone covers the core hours from ten to three. Since then, the number of sick days employees take has dropped by a quarter compared with the same months a year earlier. Flexible hours, then, have cut down on absences.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the manager's argument?`,
      opts:[`The company raised salaries for most employees at the same time hours became flexible.`,`Most employees said in a survey that they like being able to set their own hours.`,`The winter after the change brought an unusually mild flu season to the region.`,`The drop in sick days was largest among employees who changed their hours the most.`,`Some employees chose to keep exactly the hours they had worked before the change.`],
      a:3, why:`Strengthen. If the employees who used the flexibility most showed the biggest drop, more of the cause went with more of the effect, which supports the causal claim. The raise and the mild flu season are rival causes, which weaken. Liking the policy doesn't show it cut absences. Some employees keeping their hours doesn't connect the policy to the drop.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Prosecutor: Whoever burglarized the Avery Street hardware shop last month got in by breaking the small back window and reaching through to the latch. The defendant's fingerprints were found on the inside of that window's glass. So the defendant is the burglar.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the prosecutor's argument?`,
      opts:[`The defendant had been a customer in the shop many times over the years.`,`The owner washed that window inside and out hours before the break in.`,`The back window of the shop faces an alley that has no streetlights at all.`,`Nothing of great value was taken from the shop on the night of the break in.`,`The defendant's fingerprints were on file from an application for a job.`],
      a:1, why:`Strengthen. Prints fit the burglar, and they'd also fit someone who touched the glass on another day. If the window was washed clean hours before the break in, the prints had to be left after, which closes off that explanation. A frequent customer has an innocent reason for prints, which weakens. The alley, what was taken, and why his prints were on file don't touch the gap.`, task:`LR07`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Restaurant critic: Bistro Lune serves the best food in Halsey, and I'm not relying only on my own visits. On the city's most popular review site, where diners rate restaurants from one to five stars, Bistro Lune has received more five star reviews than any other restaurant in town.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the critic's argument?`,
      opts:[`Bistro Lune's head chef trained for several years at well known restaurants abroad.`,`The site lets its users sort restaurants by neighborhood and by price range.`,`Bistro Lune is among the most expensive restaurants anywhere in Halsey.`,`Several other restaurants in Halsey have also received many five star reviews.`,`Bistro Lune has been on the site far longer than most and has many more reviews in all.`],
      a:4, why:`Weaken. A count of five star reviews favors whoever has the most reviews. If Bistro Lune simply has far more reviews in total, its count could be high even if its share of top ratings is ordinary. The chef's training would, if anything, strengthen. How users can sort the listings says nothing about food. Price is irrelevant, and other restaurants having many top reviews is consistent with Lune having the most.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`A survey of members of the Ridgeview Fitness Center found that 85 percent of them exercise at least four days a week. The center's owner concluded that most adults in the town of Ridgeview exercise at least four days a week.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the owner's conclusion?`,
      opts:[`The survey asked members how many days a week they exercise but not for how long.`,`Adults who exercise often are much more likely than other adults to join a fitness center.`,`Ridgeview has several public parks with walking and running trails open all year.`,`The fitness center has more members than any other gym in the town of Ridgeview.`,`Some members of the center also exercise at home on days when they don't visit it.`],
      a:1, why:`Weaken. The owner generalizes from members to every adult in town. If frequent exercisers are the ones who join, members are an unrepresentative sample, and their habits say little about everyone else. How long people exercise, the parks, the gym's size, and home workouts don't make the sample any less representative.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`Psychologist: In several studies, children who own pets have scored higher on standard tests of empathy than children of the same age who don't own pets. Caring for an animal asks a child to notice another creature's needs. Owning a pet, therefore, helps children become more empathetic.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the psychologist's argument?`,
      opts:[`Most of the pets owned by children in the studies were dogs or cats.`,`The empathy tests were given at school by teachers rather than at home.`,`Children high in empathy are far likelier to ask for a pet.`,`Children with pets tend to spend more time outdoors than other children.`,`Some children without pets scored higher than some children with pets.`],
      a:2, why:`Weaken. If empathetic children are the ones who ask for pets, the empathy may come first and the pet second, so owning a pet needn't be the cause. The kind of pet and where the test was given don't bear on the direction of cause. Nothing ties time outdoors to empathy, so it isn't a rival cause as given. A few children without pets outscoring a few with pets doesn't touch the difference in averages.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Defense lawyer: My client couldn't have been the person who broke into the Corliss warehouse. His employer's time clock shows that he clocked out of his shift across town at 11:55 P.M., and the warehouse alarm, which records the exact minute it's set off, went off at midnight.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the lawyer's argument?`,
      opts:[`At that hour, the warehouse is a four minute drive from his workplace.`,`The employer's time clock was checked last month and keeps accurate time.`,`The client has never before been charged with a crime of any kind.`,`The warehouse alarm sounds whenever any outside door is opened.`,`Two coworkers say they saw the client leave just after his shift ended.`],
      a:0, why:`Weaken. The argument assumes five minutes wasn't enough time to get from work to the warehouse. A four minute drive makes it possible. An accurate clock strengthens the lawyer's timeline. His record, how the alarm works, and coworkers seeing him leave don't make the trip any more possible.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Columnist: Opponents of the proposed downtown stadium predict that it will snarl traffic. But many of these same people opposed the convention center fifteen years ago, and the convention center has been a great success. Their prediction about the stadium, then, should be dismissed.<br><br>The reasoning in the columnist's argument is flawed because the argument`,
      opts:[`fails to say how much the proposed stadium would cost the city to build`,`treats a stadium's popularity as evidence that it won't affect traffic`,`presumes that traffic downtown is worse than in other parts of the city`,`rejects a prediction since its makers were wrong about another matter`,`relies on a comparison between two buildings that serve the same purpose`],
      a:3, why:`Flaw. Being wrong about the convention center doesn't make the opponents wrong about traffic from a stadium. The argument dismisses a claim because of its source's record on a different question. Cost and the stadium's popularity never come up, nothing is said about other parts of the city, and the argument never claims the two buildings serve the same purpose.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Critic: The study the school board keeps citing, which claims that the Spark reading program raises test scores, looked at students from only two schools, both in the same suburb. A finding that narrow can't be trusted. So the Spark program does not raise test scores.<br><br>The critic's reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it`,
      opts:[`fails to say which two schools the study's students came from`,`assumes test scores are the only measure of a reading program's value`,`relies on a study paid for by the program's own developers`,`presumes students at the two schools were unusually strong readers`,`takes a weakness in a claim's evidence to show the claim is false`],
      a:4, why:`Flaw. A small sample makes the study weaker evidence that the program works. It doesn't show the program fails. The critic moves from "not well supported" to "false." Which schools they were, other measures of value, who paid, and how strong the readers were aren't part of the critic's reasoning.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Bookstore owner: Last fall, after we moved our cookbooks from the shelves at the back of the store to the table by the front door, cookbook sales rose sharply. So if we move our gardening books to that table this spring, their sales will rise too.<br><br>The owner's argument depends on the assumption that`,
      opts:[`gardening books will sell at least as well on the table as the cookbooks did`,`the rise in cookbook sales was due at least partly to the new spot`,`most customers who come into the store enter it through the front door`,`no other section of the store would gain from a move to that table`,`cookbook sales will stay high once the cookbooks leave the front table`],
      a:1, why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: the cookbook rise had nothing to do with the table, say a popular cooking show started that month. Then the move tells the owner nothing about gardening books, and the argument fails. Selling as well as the cookbooks is more than the argument needs. Deny that most customers use the front door and some still might, so the argument survives. Other sections and future cookbook sales are beside the point.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`In Brenton, surveys have long found that the fare is one of the main reasons people give for not riding the city's buses. Yet after the city cut bus fares in half at the start of last year, the number of people riding its buses fell over the year.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,
      opts:[`Brenton's fares were already lower than those of most cities its size before the fare cut.`,`Many people who ride Brenton's buses say they would keep riding if fares went up.`,`To pay for it, buses on the busiest routes now come every forty minutes, not fifteen.`,`The population of Brenton grew slightly over the same year that fares were cut.`,`Fare is named more often than any other reason in the city's surveys of nonriders.`],
      a:2, why:`Resolve the paradox. If the fare cut came with much less frequent service on the busiest routes, riders could leave even though the fare fell, because waiting is a cost too. Low fares before the cut, loyal riders, and the fare's place in the surveys don't explain a drop. A growing population makes the drop harder to explain, not easier.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`According to the testimony, what did Galvan do after he got between the two men?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`told the defendant to go home and cool off`,`called the police from the phone at the bar`,`asked the other man to leave the bar at once`,`walked the defendant out to his truck in back`,`cut both men off and closed out their tabs`],
      a:0, why:`Specific detail. He says, "I told Luis to go home and cool off. He went out the back." He mentions no call to police, says nothing about the other man leaving or about tabs, and never says he went outside.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Galvan would be most likely to agree with which one of the following statements?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`The defendant was the only man in the bar that night in a red jacket.`,`The other man in the fight was the one who started the trouble.`,`He saw the defendant in the parking lot after a quarter to eleven.`,`His testimony about the first punch was not easy for him to give.`,`The defendant was a stranger to him before the night of the fight.`],
      a:3, why:`Would agree with. Asked if he's sure, he says, "I'm sure. I wish I wasn't." That's a witness who doesn't want his own answer. He says a lot of them had the red jacket on, so the defendant wasn't the only one. He never says who started it, never puts the defendant in the lot, and says the defendant had come in for years.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Zebras' stripes evolved mainly to help them shed heat in the hottest parts of Africa.`,`A single study in 2014 disproved every older explanation of why zebras are striped.`,`Biting flies are the most common cause of disease and death among wild zebras.`,`Naturalists were careless observers whose stories about stripes had no basis at all.`,`Testing what each idea predicts has favored the biting fly answer, though it isn't settled.`],
      a:4, why:`Main idea. The passage moves from untested stories to tests of what each explanation predicts, shows the biting fly hypothesis gaining support, grants that heat is still argued, and closes on the value of predictions. Heat is a contested alternative, not the conclusion. The 2014 study gave one idea a lead and didn't disprove the rest. Disease is a detail, and the naturalists' ideas are called plausible.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Which one of the following, if true, would most strengthen the passage's claim that the difference in landings lay in the pattern itself?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Horses in plain coats swish their tails at flies more often than zebras do in the wild.`,`Horseflies landed about as rarely on striped cloth on a fence post as on striped horse coats.`,`Horseflies are drawn from a distance by the carbon dioxide that large animals breathe out.`,`Zebras kept in zoos are bitten by flies about as often as horses kept in the same zoos.`,`Biting flies are more active in the hottest hours of the day than in the early morning.`],
      a:1, why:`Strengthen or weaken a passage claim. If striped cloth with no animal under it turns flies away about as well, smell and behavior can't be what made the difference, which is exactly the passage's claim. Tail swishing compares plain coated horses with wild zebras, so it says nothing about the same horses in two coats. Carbon dioxide explains why flies come close, not why they fail to land. Zoo zebras being bitten as often as horses would weaken the claim. Time of day is irrelevant.`, task:`RC13`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`The author would be most likely to agree with which one of the following statements?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`The heat hypothesis has now been shown conclusively to be false.`,`Large predators played no part whatever in the evolution of stripes.`,`An idea that could be proved wrong earns more trust when it isn't.`,`Correlations across species tell us nothing useful about function.`,`The older explanations were each refuted in turn by separate tests.`],
      a:2, why:`Would agree with. The last paragraph says a hypothesis that predicts something "can turn out wrong, and that is exactly what makes it worth believing when it doesn't." The author says nobody should call the case closed, and that tests found little or no cooling, which isn't conclusive. The predator maps "matched poorly," which isn't no part at all. The 2014 correlation gave one idea a lead. And the older ideas were "outgrown," not refuted one at a time.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`It can be inferred from the passage that in the experiment with striped coats, the coats`,
      opts:[`made the horses less likely than before to be bitten on any part of the body`,`were tested only on horses kept in the hottest part of the zebras' range`,`changed the color of the horses' heads as well as the rest of their bodies`,`didn't work by keeping flies from coming near the horses that wore them`,`were worn by zebras as well as by horses so the two could be compared`],
      a:3, why:`Inference. The horses' uncovered heads were landed on about as often as before, so flies were still coming to the horses. The coats cut landings on the body, not visits. The heads were uncovered, so "any part of the body" and the change to the heads are both contradicted. The passage says nothing about where the horses were kept or about zebras wearing coats.`, passage:`p2`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Wednesday, January 27, seven. She sat two steps up from the bottom now. Teo's shop vac stood by the washer, and every morning the concrete under the tub had a dark edge again, whatever she told it.`,
    `Recording first. Then, no hello: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Bishop takes your knight."</span>`,
    `The board sat on the step beside her, where it sat every Wednesday. She lifted her knight off and put his bishop on its square. Either of two pawns could take it back. A child could see it.`,
    `He didn't ask about the furnace. He didn't ask about anything. First Wednesday in ten years he didn't ask, in any of his ways. She waited for it. It didn't come. So she asked hers, the one on the bottom of Ana's board, flat, in Ana's voice. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Pa. When you left that night. Did you stop anywhere?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"No."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Not for gas. Nothing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Straight home, mija. I told you."</span>`,
    `He'd told her. He'd told everybody. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Your move,"</span> he said.`,
    `She had the knight in her hand. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Next week,"</span> she said.`,
    ((s.flags.promisedhim||G.promisedhim)
      ?`The line hissed. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You made me a promise quicker than that,"</span> he said. Then nothing, so long she checked whether they'd been cut off.`
      :`The line hissed. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You never think about it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I'm thinking about it."</span> A long breath on his end. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Okay."</span>`),
    `The lady came on to say there was one minute left. Neither of them used it.`,
    `Thursday, January 28, Bianca dropped Lupe off at half past five, on her way to work. Teo had a bus down on Colfax. <span class="said" data-who="Bianca">"He'll get her by eight thirty. She's had a snack. She'll tell you she hasn't."</span> On the porch she turned back. <span class="said" data-who="Bianca">"He was out in the truck again Sunday night. First time since Christmas."</span> She looked at Dani as if Dani might know why. Dani had told him about Ronnie on Sunday. She didn't say so.`,
    `Lupe found the board before her coat was off. The knight lay on its side next to it. <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"That's my horse."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"That's Grandpa's game."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"Why's it lying down?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Grandpa took it."</span> Lupe put her chin on the table, level with the pieces. She touched the pawn beside the bishop with one finger. <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"Can't this one get him?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"It can."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"So get him back."</span>`,
    `Dani didn't. Lupe gave her Teo's look and went back to drawing.`,
    `Teo came at twenty to nine, grease to the elbows, and stood in the doorway while Lupe told him everything, the snack she hadn't had, the horse, the pawn, and that Tía wouldn't. He looked down at the board. Their father had taught them both on the garage sale board at this table, with a salt shaker for a rook. Teo had been ten the last time he sat down to it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He used to do that to me,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Do what?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Give you something easy to take. So you're busy taking it."</span> He zipped Lupe's coat to her chin. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You quit looking at the rest of the board."</span>`,
    `When they were gone she took the envelope out of the prep book. MAYBE HE DID IT across the top, NEEDED and ENOUGH under it. Under her own capitals, in smaller ones, she wrote HE SWUNG FIRST.`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`Then one more line, smaller still, because Ana would have stood over her till she did. THAT'S ALL RONNIE SAW.`
      :`She started to write what Ana would have wanted under it, the line about what Ronnie saw and didn't, and the pen stopped, and she let it.`),
    `She left his bishop on her knight's square and turned off the kitchen light.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch04.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`What She Saw`
});
